#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Makes the walking data of Navigation's routes from the game's own files: where a player can walk on each terrain
tile of the continents and of Zephras Isle (its own map, 2991; around it open sky, the terrain's holes), as staging
for Core's set Walking (Doc/ideas/Navigation.md, *The walking data*).

What is walkable is read from what the game itself collides with, sampled every SAMPLE yards (256 samples along a
tile's side):

  the ground      each map chunk's heights (the game's four triangles a cell), but where the terrain has a hole (a
                  cave's mouth, a building's cellar); where the game's triangle under a sample is too steep (over
                  SLOPE degrees) it is no floor, only a surface
  the buildings   every collision triangle of every building the object files place (WMO group files: their
                  vertices, indices and each triangle's flags), turned, tilted and moved where the game places it;
                  a triangle cut by each column of samples gives a span of height there (as a voxel field is made),
                  too steep (its slope in yards over SLOPE degrees) a wall
  the doodads     the same of every doodad (M2 model: its collision triangles) the object files place and of every
                  doodad of the sets a placed building shows (its first, global set and the placement's own), placed
                  where the building puts it: fences, rocks and tree trunks are walls, piers, decks and their stairs
                  floors. A doodad no wider than SMALL (a torch, a barrel) is left out, one walks round it; what a
                  doodad of the object files holds wholly under the ground is inside the earth (a tree's roots)
  the water       where water stands deeper than DEEP over a floor with nothing between them, that floor is under
                  water, and the water's surface is a floor to swim on; magma and slime close what lies under them.
                  The buildings' own water, magma and slime (their parts' liquids) are the ground's: the canals of
                  Stormwind, the Undercity's slime, Ironforge's forge

In each column the spans are joined where they touch; the top of a span is a floor when what made it is not too
steep and nothing stands within ROOM yards above it (a floor under a low roof or inside a wall is none) but the next
piece of the same stair or ramp passing up through the sample (within STAIR yards). A floor on the ground is land, one
on a building or a doodad a building's (BUILT: where the game's indoors is, Navigation looks for the player). A wall takes
a sample's floor only where it comes into the middle of its square (CORE); off the middle it stands between the
sample and the next one on each side whose way it crosses (from the sample's middle to that side's), so a door or a
bridge narrower than two samples stays open. Two floors side by side are one way when their heights differ by at
most CLIMB and no wall stands between them: none rising more than STEP over the higher floor under its ROOM (a wall,
a railing; a sill does not), none rising more than LEDGE between the two (a ledge; a stair's steps do not).

The floors are joined into rectangles of floors that are all one way with each other (land or water, never both), at
most WIDEST samples a side: few and large in open land, many and small in towns, caves and on mountains. Each
rectangle keeps its height, its kind and the openings along its sides into its neighbours (on its own tile and the
next): every floor beside its edge one way with its own, so each opening is found from both. A floor is one way with
another surface's beside it only where the way passes through no other floor (off a deck onto the ground beside it,
not onto the ground under it), but where a tile's floors would so come apart one such way is kept between the two
parts (a hall whose door is a wall in the data, reached under its floor). A surface's rectangles end where one of its
floors is one way with another surface's floor beside it while the surface goes on there (the ground under a deck's or
a stair's edge): an opening lies on the facing sides of both its rectangles, as the game reads it from either. Every
rectangle that can be walked to from another has the same region number, so the game can tell at once that a place
cannot be reached on foot. A region's number holds within its map (the game compares them only there): numbered in the
order of the tiles' keys, so a run gives the same numbers again. A region smaller than SMALLEST samples (a roof, a
ledge) is left out, and one with the sky over none of its floors (a cellar the data closes: nobody gets in or out) or
with most of its floors buried (the ground DEPTH yards and more under a building: Stormwind's streets over the
ground), but one a lift or a portal lands on (the floor nearest its end within LANDING yards, its height within RISE,
as Navigation finds its landing: Freewind Post's mesa, the Wizard's Sanctum's rooms).

  dev/data/staging/walking.jsonl    one line per tile with ground: set Walking, key the tile's key in the set Tiles
                                    (continent * 4096 + row * 64 + column), fields map (the continent), links (the
                                    tiles around it that have ground) and block (below)

A block is CBOR (the game reads it with C_EncodingUtil.DeserializeCBOR), deflated (DecompressString) and in base64
(DecodeBase64): an array of the format's number (FORMAT), the surfaces, the rectangles and the openings, each a flat
array of whole numbers:

  surfaces     per surface (its rectangles come one after the other, in this order) 3 numbers: how many rectangles
               it has, its kind (1 land, 2 water, 3 a building's floor) and its region
  rectangles   per rectangle 5 numbers: its column and row on the tile (0 to 255, from the north west), its width
               and height in samples, and its height in quarter yards
  openings     per rectangle, in order: how many numbers follow, then its openings that the game cannot work out
               itself. Two rectangles of one surface that touch are open along all their shared edge (any two
               neighbouring samples of a surface are one way), and are not written. Every other opening is
               written: one along the whole edge two rectangles share as the neighbour alone, else as -1 - its side
               (0 north, 1 east, 2 south, 3 west), the first and last sample along that side (on the rectangle's
               tile), and the neighbour. The neighbour is its index (from 0) times 5 plus where it lies (0 on the
               same tile, else 1 + the next tile's direction, as the sides). On its own tile an opening is written
               once, from the north or west of the two; into the next tile, from both.

The input, as for dev/tools/map-meta, for every map with terrain files (the continents, the isle):
  game files    a folder with adt/ (the root terrain files, <tile key>.adt), adt-obj0/ (the object files: where
                buildings and doodads stand), wmo/ (every placed building's root file <file ID>.wmo and its group
                files <file ID>_<part>.wmo) and m2/ (every model placed or shown by a building, <file ID>.m2)
  client tables a folder of the game's client tables as CSV (LiquidType, LiquidMaterial)
and the lifts and portals of the master's set Travel (assets/data/Travel: each end's place and height).
Options: --maps <map>[,<map>...] those maps' tiles only (the staging file's lines of the other maps are kept: a map
added, as 2991, leaves the others' lines alone), --only <key>[,<key>...] some tiles only (with their neighbours'
edges), for the pictures and the report alone: no staging, since a region's number is given over the tiles of the
run and a few tiles' numbers cannot agree with the master's (regions), --pictures <folder> a picture of each tile's
rectangles, --processes N (default PROCESSES: a full run with many workers reached the memory the app's cgroup may
hold, and the kernel killed it and the app), --memory GB the memory its cgroup may hold (default MEMORY; the app's scope when started from a session, the other sessions counted too): past it the
run stops, its workers ended, with no staging written, to be run one map at a time.

Needs Python with NumPy (and Pillow for the pictures). About the game's build of the set Tiles (BUILD).

Once a run is taken in (dev/tools/data import dev/data/staging/walking.jsonl --apply --take), the set's coarse layer is
made anew from the master's blocks: dev/tools/walk-gates, its staging taken in the same way (dev/tests/walk.py fails on a
layer not made from the blocks as they are).

Usage:  dev/tools/walk-data <game files> <client tables> [--maps maps] [--only keys] [--pictures folder] [--processes N] [--memory GB]
"""
import base64
import json
import math
import os
import struct
import sys
import zlib
from importlib.machinery import SourceFileLoader
from importlib.util import module_from_spec, spec_from_loader
from multiprocessing import Pool
from pathlib import Path

import numpy as np

ROOT = Path(__file__).resolve().parents[2]
STAGING = ROOT / "dev" / "data" / "staging" / "walking.jsonl"
TRAVEL = ROOT / "assets" / "data" / "Travel" / "Travel.jsonl"  # the master's set Travel: its lifts
BUILD = 70707          # the client build the game files are of (the set Tiles' build)
PROCESSES = 3          # workers: a full run with 10 reached the memory the app's cgroup may hold (24 GB) and the kernel
                       # killed the run and the app; one map at a time with 3 peaked near 20 GB (--processes)
MEMORY = 22            # GB the run's cgroup may hold (--memory; from a session: the app's whole scope): past it the run stops
TASKS = 50             # tiles a worker reads before a fresh one takes its place: its memory given back
CACHE = 512 * 2 ** 20  # bytes a per-process cache (models, buildings, pools) may hold: past it the oldest entries go

GRID = 64              # tiles along each side of a continent
TILE = 1600 / 3        # a tile's side in yards
SIDE = 256             # samples along a tile's side
SAMPLE = TILE / SIDE   # yards between two samples (2.08)
HALF = GRID / 2 * TILE # yards from a continent's middle to its edges

SLOPE = 50             # degrees: steeper ground or building floor is no floor
ROOM = 2.0             # yards a floor needs free above it
CLIMB = 2.6            # yards two floors side by side may differ and still be one way (a little over the slope)
CORE = 0.3             # samples: a wall in the square of this side round a sample's middle leaves it no floor
STEP = 0.5             # yards a wall between two floors side by side may rise over the higher (a sill, a curb)
LEDGE = 1.5            # yards a wall between two floors side by side may rise between them (a stair's step)
DEEP = 1.6             # yards of water over a floor that make it one to swim over
JOIN = 0.25            # yards: spans of a column nearer than this are one
STAIR = 1.0            # yards: a sloped walkable piece this near over a floor is the same stair or ramp going on
DEPTH = 10             # yards: the ground with a building this far over it is buried (the ground under a city)
WIDEST = 32            # samples: a rectangle's longest side
SMALLEST = 3000        # samples: a region smaller than this (a roof, a ledge) is left out, but one a lift or a
                       # portal lands on
SMALL = 2.0            # yards: a doodad no wider than this either way (a torch, a barrel, a thin trunk) is left out:
                       # one walks round it, where it would take a sample or close the way between two
LANDING = 20           # yards from a lift's or a portal's end its landing may lie (Navigation's Lifts.LANDING and
                       # Planner.ARRIVE)
RISE = 12              # yards a landing may lie over or under the lift's end (Navigation's Walk.RISE)
LEVEL = 3              # a yard up or down counts as this many across when a landing is chosen (Navigation's
                       # Walk.Landing): one gets off level with the car, beside its shaft, not onto the roof over it
# A square of the ground (outside buildings) may take this share of steep samples at its edges, by its side: the
# edges of walkable ground, ragged at two yards, make no crowd of small rectangles.
TAKEN = {2: 0.5, 4: 0.75, 8: 0.85, 16: 0.9, 32: 0.95}
THIN = 0.5             # yards: the ground's surface is a span this thick
UP = math.cos(math.radians(SLOPE))
STEEP = math.tan(math.radians(SLOPE)) * SAMPLE  # height over one sample past which the ground is too steep
YARDS = np.array([SAMPLE, SAMPLE, 1.0])          # the samples' space (columns, rows, height) in yards
LAND, WATER, BUILT = 1, 2, 3  # a floor's kind: the ground, a water's surface, a building's or a doodad's floor
SKY, COVERED, BURIED = 1, 0, -1  # what is over a floor: the sky, something, a building DEPTH yards or more (the ground)
FORMAT = 1             # the blocks' format, as Navigation's Walk.lua reads it


def art():
    """dev/tools/terrain-art's readers of the terrain files, read without running the tool."""
    loader = SourceFileLoader("terrain_art", str(ROOT / "dev" / "tools" / "terrain-art"))
    module = module_from_spec(spec_from_loader("terrain_art", loader))
    loader.exec_module(module)
    return module


ART = art()
pieces = ART.pieces


# -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  The ground and the water
# -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
def holes(data, at):
    """A map chunk's holes: 8 x 8 cells, True where the terrain has none."""
    flags = struct.unpack_from("<I", data, at)[0]
    cells = np.zeros((8, 8), bool)
    if flags & 0x10000:  # the fine holes: a byte a row of cells
        for row in range(8):
            byte = data[at + 0x14 + row]
            for column in range(8):
                cells[row, column] = bool(byte & (1 << column))
    else:  # the coarse ones: 4 x 4, each two cells a side
        bits = struct.unpack_from("<H", data, at + 0x3C)[0]
        for index in range(16):
            if bits & (1 << index):
                row, column = divmod(index, 4)
                cells[row * 2:row * 2 + 2, column * 2:column * 2 + 2] = True
    return cells


def ground(path, waters, liquids):
    """A tile's ground at every sample (SIDE x SIDE, rows from the north), where it has a hole, the water's surface
    and the surface of what closes the ground under it (magma, slime); -inf where there is none."""
    data = path.read_bytes()
    chunks, water_at = [], None
    for name, at, size in pieces(data, 0, len(data)):
        if name == b"MCNK":
            chunks.append((at, size))
        elif name == b"MH2O":
            water_at = at
    cells = ART.CHUNKS * ART.CELLS
    outer = np.zeros((cells + 1, cells + 1), np.float32)
    inner = np.zeros((cells, cells), np.float32)
    hole = np.zeros((cells, cells), bool)
    for at, size in chunks:
        x, y = struct.unpack_from("<II", data, at + 4)
        base = struct.unpack_from("<f", data, at + 0x70)[0]
        hole[y * 8:y * 8 + 8, x * 8:x * 8 + 8] = holes(data, at)
        for name, start, _ in pieces(data, at + 128, at + size):
            if name == b"MCVT":
                heights = np.array(struct.unpack_from("<145f", data, start), np.float32) + base
                rows = np.pad(heights, (0, 8)).reshape(9, 17)
                outer[y * 8:y * 8 + 9, x * 8:x * 8 + 9] = rows[:, :9]
                inner[y * 8:y * 8 + 8, x * 8:x * 8 + 8] = rows[:8, 9:]
                break
    height = ART.ground(outer, inner)
    steep = steepness(outer, inner)
    hole = np.repeat(np.repeat(hole, ART.SAMPLES, 0), ART.SAMPLES, 1)
    water = np.full(height.shape, -np.inf, np.float32)
    closed = np.full(height.shape, -np.inf, np.float32)
    if water_at is not None:
        n = ART.CELLS * ART.SAMPLES
        big = lambda grid: np.repeat(np.repeat(grid, ART.SAMPLES, 0), ART.SAMPLES, 1)  # noqa: E731
        for index in range(ART.CHUNKS * ART.CHUNKS):
            first, layers, _ = struct.unpack_from("<III", data, water_at + index * 12)
            cy, cx = divmod(index, ART.CHUNKS)
            for layer in range(layers):
                kind, form, low, _, left, top, wide, high, bits, levels = struct.unpack_from(
                    "<HHffBBBBII", data, water_at + first + layer * 24)
                if wide == 0 or high == 0:
                    continue
                if kind in waters:
                    form = form if form <= 41 else waters[kind]
                else:
                    form = form if form <= 41 else liquids.get(kind, 0)
                mask = np.ones((high, wide), bool)
                if bits:
                    flags = np.frombuffer(data, np.uint8, (wide * high + 7) // 8, water_at + bits)
                    mask = np.unpackbits(flags, bitorder="little")[:wide * high].reshape(high, wide).astype(bool)
                if levels and form in (0, 1, 3):
                    corners = np.array(struct.unpack_from(f"<{(wide + 1) * (high + 1)}f", data, water_at + levels),
                                       np.float32).reshape(high + 1, wide + 1)
                else:
                    corners = np.full((high + 1, wide + 1), low, np.float32)
                fx, fy = np.tile(ART.FX, (high, wide)), np.tile(ART.FY, (high, wide))
                level = (big(corners[:-1, :-1]) * (1 - fx) * (1 - fy) + big(corners[:-1, 1:]) * fx * (1 - fy)
                         + big(corners[1:, :-1]) * (1 - fx) * fy + big(corners[1:, 1:]) * fx * fy)
                level = np.where(big(mask), level, -np.inf)
                y0, x0 = cy * n + top * ART.SAMPLES, cx * n + left * ART.SAMPLES
                target = water if kind in waters else closed
                region = target[y0:y0 + high * ART.SAMPLES, x0:x0 + wide * ART.SAMPLES]
                np.maximum(region, level, out=region)
    return height, hole, water, closed, steep


def steepness(outer, inner):
    """Whether the ground's own triangle under each sample is steeper than SLOPE (SIDE x SIDE, rows from the north):
    the game draws a cell as four triangles meeting at its inner height (terrain-art's ground), and nobody walks up
    one steeper than that, whatever the slopes around it. A sample's middle lies on the edge between two of them; it
    takes the one terrain-art's ground takes its height from (the west or the east one)."""
    side = TILE / (ART.CHUNKS * ART.CELLS)  # a cell's side in yards
    a, b, c, d, m = outer[:-1, :-1], outer[:-1, 1:], outer[1:, :-1], outer[1:, 1:], inner

    def steep(p, q, r):
        (px, py, pz), (qx, qy, qz), (rx, ry, rz) = p, q, r
        ux, uy, uz, vx, vy, vz = qx - px, qy - py, qz - pz, rx - px, ry - py, rz - pz
        nx, ny, nz = uy * vz - uz * vy, uz * vx - ux * vz, ux * vy - uy * vx
        return np.abs(nz) < UP * np.sqrt(nx * nx + ny * ny + nz * nz)

    corners = (0, 0, a), (side, 0, b), (0, side, c), (side, side, d)
    middle = (side / 2, side / 2, m)
    north, east = steep(corners[0], corners[1], middle), steep(corners[1], corners[3], middle)
    south, west = steep(corners[2], corners[3], middle), steep(corners[0], corners[2], middle)
    cells = ART.CHUNKS * ART.CELLS
    big = lambda grid: np.repeat(np.repeat(grid, ART.SAMPLES, 0), ART.SAMPLES, 1)  # noqa: E731
    found = np.where(np.tile(ART.WEST, (cells, cells)), big(west), big(east))
    found = np.where(np.tile(ART.SOUTH, (cells, cells)), big(south), found)
    return np.where(np.tile(ART.NORTH, (cells, cells)), big(north), found)


# -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  The buildings and the doodads
# -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
BUILDING, DOODAD = 1, 2


def placements(folder):
    """Everything the object files place that the game collides with, once: {continent: [placement]} with its kind
    (BUILDING, DOODAD), file, place, turn, scale and box in world yards (north, south, west, east); a building with
    the doodad sets it shows, a doodad only when its model has collision wider than SMALL (its box reaches as far
    as the model's farthest corner, whichever way it is turned)."""
    found = {}
    for path in sorted((folder / "adt-obj0").glob("*.adt")):
        continent = int(path.stem) // (GRID * GRID)
        places = found.setdefault(continent, {})
        data = path.read_bytes()
        top = chunks(data, 0, len(data))
        ranges = sets = ()
        if b"MWDR" in top and b"MWDS" in top:  # several doodad sets a building (Zephras Isle's)
            at, size = top[b"MWDR"]
            ranges = struct.unpack_from(f"<{size // 4}I", data, at)
            at, size = top[b"MWDS"]
            sets = struct.unpack_from(f"<{size // 2}H", data, at)
        at, size = top.get(b"MODF", (0, 0))
        for start in range(at, at + size, 64):
            (file, unique, px, py, pz, rx, ry, rz, lx, ly, lz, ux, uy, uz, flags, shown, _,
             scale) = struct.unpack_from("<II3f3f3f3fHHHH", data, start)
            if not flags & 0x8:
                continue
            # The first doodad set (the building's global one) is always shown, and the placement's own; with flag
            # 0x80 its own are those of a range of MWDS (MWDR: its first and last).
            own = sets[ranges[2 * shown]:ranges[2 * shown + 1] + 1] if flags & 0x80 else (shown,)
            north, south = HALF - min(lz, uz), HALF - max(lz, uz)
            west, east = HALF - min(lx, ux), HALF - max(lx, ux)
            places[(BUILDING, unique)] = dict(
                kind=BUILDING, file=file, sets=tuple(sorted({0, *own})), at=(px, py, pz), turn=(rx, ry, rz),
                scale=scale / 1024 if scale else 1, box=(north, south, west, east))
        at, size = top.get(b"MDDF", (0, 0))
        for start in range(at, at + size, 36):
            file, unique, px, py, pz, rx, ry, rz, scale, flags = struct.unpack_from("<II3f3fHH", data, start)
            if not flags & 0x40:  # a model named in MMDX instead of its file ID: none on these maps
                continue
            across, far = measure(folder, file)
            if across * scale / 1024 > SMALL:
                far *= scale / 1024
                places[(DOODAD, unique)] = dict(
                    kind=DOODAD, file=file, at=(px, py, pz), turn=(rx, ry, rz), scale=scale / 1024,
                    box=(HALF - pz + far, HALF - pz - far, HALF - px + far, HALF - px - far))
    return {continent: list(places.values()) for continent, places in found.items()}


def chunks(data, start, end):
    return {name: (at, size) for name, at, size in pieces(data, start, end)}


def group(path):
    """A group file's collision triangles in the building's own space: an (n, 3, 3) array of corners (x, y, z)."""
    data = path.read_bytes()
    top = chunks(data, 0, len(data))
    if b"MOGP" not in top:
        return np.zeros((0, 3, 3), np.float32)
    at, size = top[b"MOGP"]
    inner = chunks(data, at + 68, at + size)
    if b"MOVT" not in inner:
        return np.zeros((0, 3, 3), np.float32)
    vat, vsize = inner[b"MOVT"]
    vertices = np.frombuffer(data, np.float32, vsize // 4, vat).reshape(-1, 3)
    if b"MOVI" in inner:
        iat, isize = inner[b"MOVI"]
        indices = np.frombuffer(data, np.uint16, isize // 2, iat).astype(np.int64).reshape(-1, 3)
    elif b"MOVX" in inner:
        iat, isize = inner[b"MOVX"]
        indices = np.frombuffer(data, np.uint32, isize // 4, iat).astype(np.int64).reshape(-1, 3)
    else:
        return np.zeros((0, 3, 3), np.float32)
    if b"MOPY" in inner:
        pat, psize = inner[b"MOPY"]
        info = np.frombuffer(data, np.uint8, psize, pat).reshape(-1, 2)
        flags, material = info[:, 0].astype(np.int64), info[:, 1].astype(np.int64)
    elif b"MPY2" in inner:
        pat, psize = inner[b"MPY2"]
        info = np.frombuffer(data, np.uint16, psize // 2, pat).reshape(-1, 2)
        flags, material = info[:, 0].astype(np.int64), info[:, 1].astype(np.int64)
    else:
        flags, material = np.full(len(indices), 0x08), np.zeros(len(indices), np.int64)
    count = min(len(indices), len(flags))
    flags, indices = flags[:count], indices[:count]
    # What the game collides with: a triangle marked for collision, or one drawn that is no detail.
    collides = ((flags & 0x08) != 0) | (((flags & 0x20) != 0) & ((flags & 0x04) == 0))
    indices = indices[collides]
    indices = indices[(indices < len(vertices)).all(axis=1)]
    return vertices[indices]


class Cache(dict):
    """A per-process cache capped by the bytes of what it holds (CACHE): past it the oldest entries are dropped.
    size(value): an entry's bytes. The triangles are kept as float32, as the game's files hold them (a doodad's,
    turned into its building's space, to a ten-thousandth of a yard over a building 2000 yards across): the floors
    are judged by JOIN, a quarter yard, and placed() turns them into float64 before the world's yards are added."""

    def __init__(self, size=lambda value: value.nbytes):
        super().__init__()
        self.size, self.held = size, 0

    def put(self, key, value):
        self.held += self.size(value) - (self.size(self[key]) if key in self else 0)
        self[key] = value
        while self.held > CACHE and len(self) > 1:
            self.held -= self.size(self.pop(next(iter(self))))
        return value


_models = Cache()


def model(folder, file):
    """A model's (M2) collision triangles in its own space: an (n, 3, 3) array of corners (x, y, z), none for a model
    without (kept per process). Its header (in MD21 since the game's chunked models) holds the collision's indices
    (three a triangle) and positions."""
    if file not in _models:
        corners = np.zeros((0, 3, 3), np.float32)
        path = folder / "m2" / f"{file}.m2"
        data = path.read_bytes() if path.exists() else b""
        if data[:4] == b"MD21":
            data = data[8:8 + struct.unpack_from("<I", data, 4)[0]]
        if data[:4] == b"MD20" and len(data) >= 0xE8 and struct.unpack_from("<I", data, 4)[0] >= 264:
            count, at, many, start = struct.unpack_from("<4I", data, 0xD8)
            if count >= 3 and many and at + count * 2 <= len(data) and start + many * 12 <= len(data):
                indices = np.frombuffer(data, np.uint16, count // 3 * 3, at).astype(np.int64).reshape(-1, 3)
                vertices = np.frombuffer(data, np.float32, many * 3, start).reshape(-1, 3)
                corners = vertices[indices[(indices < many).all(axis=1)]]
        _models.put(file, corners)
    return _models[file]


_measures = {}


def measure(folder, file):
    """How wide a model's collision is (its widest side, along its own x or y) and how far its farthest corner lies
    from its middle: (0, 0) for a model without (kept per process)."""
    if file not in _measures:
        corners = model(folder, file).reshape(-1, 3)
        across = float(np.ptp(corners[:, :2], axis=0).max()) if len(corners) else 0.0
        _measures[file] = across, float(np.sqrt((corners ** 2).sum(axis=1)).max()) if len(corners) else 0.0
    return _measures[file]


def quaternion(x, y, z, w):
    """The turn of a quaternion (x, y, z, w) as a matrix."""
    length = math.sqrt(x * x + y * y + z * z + w * w) or 1.0
    x, y, z, w = x / length, y / length, z / length, w / length
    return np.array([[1 - 2 * (y * y + z * z), 2 * (x * y - z * w), 2 * (x * z + y * w)],
                     [2 * (x * y + z * w), 1 - 2 * (x * x + z * z), 2 * (y * z - x * w)],
                     [2 * (x * z - y * w), 2 * (y * z + x * w), 1 - 2 * (x * x + y * y)]])


def doodads(folder, file, sets):
    """The collision triangles of the doodads a building shows, in its own space: the MODD entries of those sets
    (MODS: each set's first entry and count), each its model (MODI: the file ID of each name) scaled, turned and moved
    where the entry puts it; but those no wider than SMALL."""
    path = folder / "wmo" / f"{file}.wmo"
    data = path.read_bytes() if path.exists() else b""
    top = chunks(data, 0, len(data))
    if not all(name in top for name in (b"MODS", b"MODD", b"MODI")):
        return []
    at, size = top[b"MODI"]
    files = struct.unpack_from(f"<{size // 4}I", data, at)
    sets_at, sets_size = top[b"MODS"]
    entries_at, entries_size = top[b"MODD"]
    found = []
    for index in sets:
        if (index + 1) * 32 > sets_size:
            continue
        first, count = struct.unpack_from("<II", data, sets_at + index * 32 + 20)
        for entry in range(first, min(first + count, entries_size // 40)):
            name, x, y, z, qx, qy, qz, qw, scale = struct.unpack_from("<I3f4ff", data, entries_at + entry * 40)
            name &= 0xFFFFFF  # the top byte holds the entry's flags
            if name < len(files) and files[name] and measure(folder, files[name])[0] * scale > SMALL:
                found.append((model(folder, files[name]) @ (quaternion(qx, qy, qz, qw).T * scale) + (x, y, z)).astype(np.float32))
    return found


_groups = Cache()


def building(folder, file, sets=(0,)):
    """Every collision triangle of a building in its own space: its parts joined, and the doodads of the sets it
    shows (kept per process, CACHE bytes at most)."""
    if (file, sets) not in _groups:
        parts = [group(path) for path in sorted((folder / "wmo").glob(f"{file}_*.wmo"))]
        parts = [part for part in parts + doodads(folder, file, sets) if len(part)]
        _groups.put((file, sets), np.concatenate(parts) if parts else np.zeros((0, 3, 3), np.float32))
    return _groups[(file, sets)]


def rotation(axis, degrees):
    """A turn about an axis (0 x, 1 y, 2 z) as a matrix."""
    c, s = math.cos(math.radians(degrees)), math.sin(math.radians(degrees))
    if axis == 0:
        return np.array([[1, 0, 0], [0, c, -s], [0, s, c]])
    if axis == 1:
        return np.array([[c, 0, s], [0, 1, 0], [-s, 0, c]])
    return np.array([[c, -s, 0], [s, c, 0], [0, 0, 1]])


OWN = np.array([[1, 0, 0], [0, 0, 1], [0, -1, 0]])     # its own axes (x, y, z up) as the object files' axes
WORLD = np.array([[0, 0, -1], [-1, 0, 0], [0, 1, 0]])  # the object files' axes (x, height, z) as the world's


POURED = 4             # points along each side of a building's water tile (4.17 yards): a sample's every corner wet


def liquid(kind, flags, first):
    """The liquid type (LiquidType) of a building's part, as the game takes it: its own number (MOGP), the table's
    where the building's flags say so (MOHD 0x4; first None), else one more than it (15: one more than its first
    tile's own); of the table's first twenty each fourth is water (the part's flag 0x80000: ocean), ocean, magma,
    slime."""
    if first is not None:
        kind = first + 1 if kind == 15 else kind + 1
    if 0 < kind < 21:
        kind = (14 if flags & 0x80000 else 13, 14, 19, 20)[(kind - 1) & 3]
    return kind


_pools = Cache(lambda found: sum(points.nbytes for points, _ in found))


def pools(folder, file):
    """A building's water, magma and slime in its own space (kept per process, CACHE bytes at most): a list of
    (points (n, 3): its surface at POURED x POURED points of every tile its parts' liquids (MLIQ) cover, its liquid
    type)."""
    if file not in _pools:
        found = []
        root = folder / f"{file}.wmo"
        data = root.read_bytes() if root.exists() else b""
        top = chunks(data, 0, len(data))
        legacy = not (b"MOHD" in top and struct.unpack_from("<H", data, top[b"MOHD"][0] + 60)[0] & 0x4)
        for path in sorted(folder.glob(f"{file}_*.wmo")):
            data = path.read_bytes()
            top = chunks(data, 0, len(data))
            if b"MOGP" not in top:
                continue
            at, size = top[b"MOGP"]
            inner = chunks(data, at + 68, at + size)
            if b"MLIQ" not in inner:
                continue
            flags, kind = struct.unpack_from("<I", data, at + 8)[0], struct.unpack_from("<I", data, at + 0x34)[0]
            lat, _ = inner[b"MLIQ"]
            xverts, yverts, xtiles, ytiles = struct.unpack_from("<4i", data, lat)
            bx, by, _ = struct.unpack_from("<3f", data, lat + 16)
            count = xverts * yverts
            if xtiles <= 0 or ytiles <= 0 or xverts != xtiles + 1 or yverts != ytiles + 1:
                continue
            heights = np.frombuffer(data, "<f4", count * 2, lat + 30).reshape(yverts, xverts, 2)[..., 1]
            tiles = np.frombuffer(data, np.uint8, xtiles * ytiles, lat + 30 + 8 * count).reshape(ytiles, xtiles)
            wet = (tiles & 0x0F) != 0x0F
            if not wet.any():
                continue
            kind = liquid(kind, flags, int(tiles[wet][0] & 0x0F) if legacy else None)
            if kind <= 0:
                continue
            rows, cols = np.nonzero(wet)
            step = (np.arange(POURED) + 0.5) / POURED
            fy, fx = np.meshgrid(step, step, indexing="ij")
            fy, fx = fy.ravel(), fx.ravel()
            j, i = rows[:, None], cols[:, None]
            level = (heights[j, i] * (1 - fx) * (1 - fy) + heights[j, i + 1] * fx * (1 - fy)
                     + heights[j + 1, i] * (1 - fx) * fy + heights[j + 1, i + 1] * fx * fy)
            unit = TILE / (ART.CHUNKS * ART.CELLS)  # a water tile's side: a terrain cell's
            points = np.stack([bx + (i + fx) * unit, by + (j + fy) * unit, level], axis=-1).reshape(-1, 3)
            found.append((points.astype(np.float32), kind))
        _pools.put(file, found)
    return _pools[file]


def placed(where, corners):
    """A building's or a doodad's corners (n, 3, 3: its own x, y, z) where the game places it: (north, west, height)
    in yards. On the object files' axes (x, the height, z) the game tilts it about x by its turn's third angle, then
    about z by minus the first, then turns it about the height by the second less 90 degrees, and scales it."""
    px, py, pz = where["at"]
    rx, ry, rz = where["turn"]
    matrix = WORLD @ rotation(1, ry - 90) @ rotation(2, -rx) @ rotation(0, rz) @ OWN
    return corners @ (matrix.T * where["scale"]) + (HALF - pz, HALF - px, py)


def clip(polygon, axis, low, high):
    """A polygon ([(a, b, z), ...]) cut to low <= its axis' value <= high (Sutherland and Hodgman, twice)."""
    for bound, keep in ((low, lambda v: v >= low), (high, lambda v: v <= high)):
        out = []
        count = len(polygon)
        for index in range(count):
            p, q = polygon[index], polygon[(index + 1) % count]
            pin, qin = keep(p[axis]), keep(q[axis])
            if pin:
                out.append(p)
            if pin != qin:
                t = (bound - p[axis]) / (q[axis] - p[axis])
                out.append(tuple(p[i] + (q[i] - p[i]) * t for i in range(3)))
        polygon = out
        if not polygon:
            break
    return polygon


# The way from a sample's middle to each of its sides (0 north, 1 east, 2 south, 3 west) as a box in the sample's
# square (first and last column, first and last row, from its north west corner), a line THREAD thick; and its
# middle, CORE wide.
THREAD = 0.01
SPOKES = ((0.5 - THREAD, 0.5 + THREAD, 0.0, 0.5), (0.5, 1.0, 0.5 - THREAD, 0.5 + THREAD),
          (0.5 - THREAD, 0.5 + THREAD, 0.5, 1.0), (0.0, 0.5, 0.5 - THREAD, 0.5 + THREAD))
MIDDLE = ((1 - CORE) / 2, (1 + CORE) / 2, (1 - CORE) / 2, (1 + CORE) / 2)


def rasterize(triangles, spans, walls):
    """Each triangle (in the tile's samples: column, row, height) into the columns it covers: a span (low, high,
    walkable) in spans[(row, column)]. A triangle too steep to walk on (a wall) that keeps out of a sample's middle
    leaves its floor alone: where it crosses the way from the middle to a side, it stands on that side, (low, high)
    in walls[(row, column, side)]; so a door or a bridge narrower than two samples is not closed by its sides."""
    for triangle in triangles:
        a, b, c = triangle * YARDS  # the slope in yards, not in samples across and yards up
        normal = np.cross(b - a, c - a)
        length = math.sqrt(normal[0] ** 2 + normal[1] ** 2 + normal[2] ** 2)
        if length == 0:
            continue
        walkable = abs(normal[2]) / length >= UP
        cols, rows = triangle[:, 0], triangle[:, 1]
        r0, r1 = max(int(math.floor(rows.min())), 0), min(int(math.floor(rows.max())), SIDE - 1)
        c0, c1 = max(int(math.floor(cols.min())), 0), min(int(math.floor(cols.max())), SIDE - 1)
        if r0 > r1 or c0 > c1:
            continue
        polygon = [tuple(point) for point in triangle]
        if r0 == r1 and c0 == c1:
            put(polygon, r0, c0, walkable, spans, walls)
            continue
        for row in range(r0, r1 + 1):
            strip = clip(polygon, 1, row, row + 1)
            if len(strip) < 3:
                continue
            low = max(int(math.floor(min(p[0] for p in strip))), c0)
            high = min(int(math.floor(max(p[0] for p in strip))), c1)
            for column in range(low, high + 1):
                cell = clip(strip, 0, column, column + 1)
                if len(cell) >= 3:
                    put(cell, row, column, walkable, spans, walls)


def put(cell, row, column, walkable, spans, walls):
    """A triangle's part in one sample's column (rasterize): a span, or a wall on the sides it crosses the way to."""
    heights = [p[2] for p in cell]
    if walkable:
        spans.setdefault((row, column), []).append((min(heights), max(heights), True))
        return
    left, right = min(p[0] for p in cell) - column, max(p[0] for p in cell) - column
    top, bottom = min(p[1] for p in cell) - row, max(p[1] for p in cell) - row

    def part(box):
        if left > box[1] or right < box[0] or top > box[3] or bottom < box[2]:
            return []
        return clip(clip(cell, 0, column + box[0], column + box[1]), 1, row + box[2], row + box[3])

    if part(MIDDLE):
        spans.setdefault((row, column), []).append((min(heights), max(heights), False))
    for side, box in enumerate(SPOKES):
        crossed = part(box)
        if crossed:
            walls.setdefault((row, column, side), []).append((min(p[2] for p in crossed), max(p[2] for p in crossed)))


# -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  Floors
# -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
def smooth(grid):
    """Each value the mean of it and its eight neighbours (the edges as they are)."""
    padded = np.pad(grid, 1, mode="edge")
    total = sum(padded[1 + dr:1 + dr + SIDE, 1 + dc:1 + dc + SIDE] for dr in (-1, 0, 1) for dc in (-1, 0, 1))
    return total / 9


def roomy(joined, index):
    """Whether a column's span (joined: its spans, lowest first) has room for a floor on top: ROOM free over it, or
    over it only the next piece of the same stair or ramp passing up through the sample (walkable, within STAIR yards
    over it, one of the two sloped: taller than JOIN), which is no ceiling."""
    if index + 1 == len(joined):
        return True
    below, top, _ = joined[index]
    low, high, walkable = joined[index + 1]
    if low - top >= ROOM:
        return True
    return walkable and low - top <= STAIR and (top - below > JOIN or high - low > JOIN)


def floors(height, hole, water, closed, spans, walls=(), steep=None):
    """Every column's floors, lowest first: {(row, column): [(height, kind, open)]}, open where nothing stands over the
    floor (the sky over it: the ground alone, a column's highest span, a water's surface with nothing over it); the
    columns with only the ground as
    one floor array (NaN where none) for speed; and where the ground is only too steep (no hole, no building, no
    water): a speck of it may be walked over (rectangles)."""
    # The ground over three samples a side: its small bumps make no steep specks, and the slope it is judged by
    # and the heights its floors are joined by are the same.
    height = smooth(height.astype(np.float64))
    if steep is None:  # no triangles given (made-up ground): its slope between the samples
        gy, gx = np.gradient(height, edge_order=1)
        steep = np.maximum(np.abs(gy), np.abs(gx)) > STEEP
    plain = np.where(~hole, height, np.nan)
    walk = ~hole & ~steep
    # ceiling: the top of a column's highest span; overs: under each floor's column, where the next span over it
    # begins (none: inf), which keeps the water over it off it (a tunnel under a river).
    found, ceiling, overs = {}, {}, {}
    for (row, column), column_spans in spans.items():
        g = math.nan
        if not hole[row, column]:
            g = float(height[row, column])
            column_spans = column_spans + [(g - THIN, g, bool(walk[row, column]))]
        column_spans.sort()
        joined = []
        for low, high, walkable in column_spans:
            if joined and low <= joined[-1][1] + JOIN:
                last = joined[-1]
                if high > last[1] + JOIN:
                    joined[-1] = [last[0], high, walkable]
                elif high >= last[1] - JOIN:
                    joined[-1] = [last[0], max(high, last[1]), last[2] or walkable]
            else:
                joined.append([low, high, walkable])
        # A floor on the ground is land; one on a building, a building's (BUILT).
        here, over = [], []
        for index, (_, top, ok) in enumerate(joined):
            if ok and roomy(joined, index):
                land = abs(top - g) <= JOIN
                above = joined[index + 1][0] if index + 1 < len(joined) else math.inf
                here.append((top, LAND if land else BUILT,
                             SKY if above == math.inf else BURIED if land and above - top > DEPTH else COVERED))
                over.append(above)
        found[(row, column)], overs[(row, column)] = here, over
        ceiling[(row, column)] = joined[-1][1]
    # The ground alone where no building is.
    alone = np.where(walk, plain, np.nan)
    speck = steep & ~hole
    for key in found:
        alone[key] = np.nan
        speck[key] = False
    # The water: a floor under deep water is one to swim over, at the water's surface; what lies under magma
    # or slime is closed. Only where nothing stands between the floor and the surface: under a floor of its own
    # (a building's pool over the ground under the city) a floor stays dry.
    rows, columns = np.nonzero(np.isfinite(water) | np.isfinite(closed))
    for row, column in zip(rows.tolist(), columns.tolist()):
        key = (row, column)
        here, over = found.get(key), overs.get(key)
        if here is None:
            g = alone[row, column]
            here = [(float(g), LAND, True)] if np.isfinite(g) else []
            over = [math.inf] * len(here)
            alone[row, column] = np.nan
        surface, shut = float(water[row, column]), float(closed[row, column])
        shut_in = [floor[0] < shut and ceiling_at >= shut - JOIN for floor, ceiling_at in zip(here, over)]
        deep = [floor[0] < surface - DEEP and ceiling_at >= surface - JOIN for floor, ceiling_at in zip(here, over)]
        kept = [floor for floor, a, b in zip(here, shut_in, deep) if not a and not b]
        swum = any(b and not a for a, b in zip(shut_in, deep)) or (not here and math.isfinite(surface))
        if math.isfinite(surface) and surface > shut and (swum or hole[row, column] or not here):
            if not any(abs(floor[0] - surface) < ROOM for floor in kept):
                kept.append((surface, WATER, SKY if surface >= ceiling.get(key, -math.inf) - JOIN else COVERED))
        kept.sort()
        found[key] = kept
        speck[row, column] = False
    # The columns a building or a doodad reaches into near the ground (or where the ground has a hole): a canopy
    # high over it or what lies under it leaves the ground's own rules alone.
    built = np.zeros((SIDE, SIDE), bool)

    def reaches(row, column, parts):
        g = height[row, column]
        return hole[row, column] or any(high >= g - CLIMB and low <= g + ROOM for low, high, *_ in parts)

    for (row, column), column_spans in spans.items():
        built[row, column] = reaches(row, column, column_spans)
    for (row, column, _), column_walls in walls.items():
        built[row, column] |= reaches(row, column, column_walls)
    return alone, found, np.where(speck, height, np.nan), built


def floors_of(alone, found, row, column):
    """A column's floors, lowest first."""
    here = found.get((row, column))
    if here is not None:
        return here
    g = alone[row, column]
    return [(float(g), LAND, True)] if math.isfinite(g) else []


def near(a, b):
    """Whether two floors side by side are one way: their heights differ by at most CLIMB."""
    return abs(a[0] - b[0]) <= CLIMB


SIDES = ((0, -1, 0), (1, 0, 1), (2, 1, 0), (3, 0, -1))  # side, row step, column step: north, east, south, west


def stands(walls, a, b):
    """Whether one of the walls ((low, high), ...) on the way between two floors side by side, at heights a and b,
    stands in it: rising more than STEP over the higher floor and lower than the ROOM over it (a wall, a railing),
    or rising more than LEDGE between them (a ledge: no step)."""
    low, top = min(a, b), max(a, b)
    return any((high > top + STEP and bottom < top + ROOM) or min(high, top) - max(bottom, low) > LEDGE
               for bottom, high in walls)


def walled(walls, row, column, side, a, b):
    """Whether a wall stands between two floors side by side, a (row, column's height) and b (the next sample's on
    that side): on the way from either middle to their shared side (rasterize)."""
    if not walls:
        return False
    _, dr, dc = SIDES[side]
    return (stands(walls.get((row, column, side), ()), a, b)
            or stands(walls.get((row + dr, column + dc, (side + 2) % 4), ()), a, b))


def through(here, there, a, b):
    """Whether the way between two floors side by side, at heights a (one of here: its sample's floors' heights) and
    b (of there), passes through another floor: where the higher one goes on into the lower one's sample over it (of
    that sample's floors the one nearest the higher's height, as a surface goes on, lies over the lower; a tie too).
    Off a deck one steps onto the ground beside it, not onto the ground under the deck's next sample, nor from a
    stair's step onto the ground under the next step; the ground going on under a low roof is the ground."""
    if a > b:
        here, there, a, b = there, here, b, a
    return any(z > a and abs(z - b) <= b - a for z in here if z != a)


# -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  Rectangles
# -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
def surfaces(columns, walls=None):
    """The floors in surfaces: each the floors of one kind that are one way with each other, at most one in a column
    (the ground, a cave's floor under it, a bridge's deck over a river, a building's storey), found by a flood
    from floor to floor, the neighbour's floor nearest in height taken. A floor joins only when it is one way
    with every neighbour of the surface around it, so any two neighbouring samples of a surface are one way
    (the game takes two rectangles of one surface that touch as open along all their edge), no wall between them
    (walled). A list of {(row, column): index}."""
    taken = set()
    found = []
    for row in range(SIDE):
        for column in range(SIDE):
            for index in range(len(columns[row][column])):
                if (row, column, index) in taken:
                    continue
                cells = {(row, column): index}
                taken.add((row, column, index))
                queue = [(row, column, index)]
                while queue:
                    r, c, i = queue.pop()
                    floor = columns[r][c][i]
                    for _, dr, dc in SIDES:
                        nr, nc = r + dr, c + dc
                        if not (0 <= nr < SIDE and 0 <= nc < SIDE) or (nr, nc) in cells:
                            continue
                        pick, gap = -1, None
                        around = [(side, columns[nr + sr][nc + sc][cells[(nr + sr, nc + sc)]])
                                  for side, sr, sc in SIDES if (nr + sr, nc + sc) in cells]
                        for j, other in enumerate(columns[nr][nc]):
                            if (nr, nc, j) in taken or other[1] != floor[1] or not all(
                                    near(other, a) and not walled(walls, nr, nc, side, other[0], a[0]) for side, a in around):
                                continue
                            distance = abs(other[0] - floor[0])
                            if gap is None or distance < gap:
                                pick, gap = j, distance
                        if pick >= 0:
                            cells[(nr, nc)] = pick
                            taken.add((nr, nc, pick))
                            queue.append((nr, nc, pick))
                found.append(cells)
    return found


def rectangles(alone, found, specks, built, walls=None, kept=frozenset()):
    """The tile's floors joined into rectangles: a list of [column, row, width, height, z, kind], owner
    {(row, column, index): rectangle} for every floor, and every column's floors.

    Each surface (surfaces) is cut alike: squares of WIDEST on the tile's grid, halved until all their floors are
    one way with each other (a quadtree), then neighbours of the same size side by side joined while no longer
    than WIDEST. Outside buildings a square of two samples a side is taken whole when at least two of its
    samples are of the surface and the rest is ground only too steep (no hole; no floor of its own), walked
    over at the square's mean height: the land's small bumps would else cut it into single samples. Openings
    link floors of any surfaces that meet (openings); a surface's rectangles end where one is to lie (cuts)."""
    columns = [[floors_of(alone, found, row, column) for column in range(SIDE)] for row in range(SIDE)]
    owner, rects = {}, []
    parts = surfaces(columns, walls)
    cut = cuts(columns, parts, walls, kept)
    for surface, cells in enumerate(parts):
        rows = [r for r, _ in cells]
        cols = [c for _, c in cells]
        r0, c0 = min(rows) // WIDEST * WIDEST, min(cols) // WIDEST * WIDEST
        r1, c1 = max(rows) // WIDEST * WIDEST + WIDEST, max(cols) // WIDEST * WIDEST + WIDEST
        mask = np.zeros((SIDE, SIDE), bool)
        z = np.zeros((SIDE, SIDE))
        for (r, c), i in cells.items():
            mask[r, c] = True
            z[r, c] = columns[r][c][i][0]
        kind = columns[rows[0]][cols[0]][cells[(rows[0], cols[0])]][1]
        across = mask[:, :-1] & mask[:, 1:] & (np.abs(z[:, 1:] - z[:, :-1]) <= CLIMB)
        down = mask[:-1, :] & mask[1:, :] & (np.abs(z[1:, :] - z[:-1, :]) <= CLIMB)
        for r, c in cut.get((surface, 1), ()):
            across[r, c] = False
        for r, c in cut.get((surface, 2), ()):
            down[r, c] = False
        squares = []

        def split(row, column, size):
            part = mask[row:row + size, column:column + size]
            if not part.any():
                return
            inside_across = across[row:row + size, column:column + size - 1]
            inside_down = down[row:row + size - 1, column:column + size]
            if part.all() and inside_across.all() and inside_down.all():
                squares.append([column, row, size, size])
                return
            if kind == LAND and not built[row:row + size, column:column + size].any():
                # The ground's edge: a square that is mostly this surface, the rest steep ground only (no hole, no
                # floor of its own), all its own samples one way with their neighbours in it, is taken whole, the
                # steep samples walked over at the square's mean height. A steep sample's own height is left:
                # what lies around the square is one way with it only by the square's height, so no cliff is
                # walked down through it.
                count = int(part.sum())
                odd = [(row + r, column + c) for r, c in zip(*np.nonzero(~part))]
                specky = all(np.isfinite(specks[r, c]) and not columns[r][c] for r, c in odd)
                if size == 2 and count == 1 and specky:
                    return  # a lone sample among steep ground: nobody walks there
                both_across = part[:, :-1] & part[:, 1:]
                both_down = part[:-1, :] & part[1:, :]
                mean = float(z[row:row + size, column:column + size][part].mean()) if count else 0.0
                # Each steep sample taken must be one way, at that height, with the surface's samples around the
                # square (see surfaces).
                fits = all(abs(z[ar, ac] - mean) <= CLIMB for r, c in odd
                           for ar, ac in ((r - 1, c), (r + 1, c), (r, c - 1), (r, c + 1))
                           if 0 <= ar < SIDE and 0 <= ac < SIDE and mask[ar, ac]
                           and not (row <= ar < row + size and column <= ac < column + size))
                if (count >= TAKEN[size] * size * size and specky and fits and inside_across[both_across].all()
                        and inside_down[both_down].all()):
                    for r, c in odd:
                        columns[r][c] = [(mean, LAND, True)]
                        cells[(r, c)] = 0
                        mask[r, c], z[r, c] = True, mean
                    squares.append([column, row, size, size])
                    return
            if size == 1:
                return
            half = size // 2
            for dr in (0, half):
                for dc in (0, half):
                    split(row + dr, column + dc, half)

        for row in range(r0, min(r1, SIDE), WIDEST):
            for column in range(c0, min(c1, SIDE), WIDEST):
                split(row, column, WIDEST)

        # Neighbours of one size joined: side by side along a row, then above each other.
        def joined(blocks, horizontal):
            blocks.sort(key=lambda b: (b[1], b[3], b[0]) if horizontal else (b[0], b[2], b[1]))
            out = []
            for b in blocks:
                last = out[-1] if out else None
                if horizontal and last and last[1] == b[1] and last[3] == b[3] and last[0] + last[2] == b[0] \
                        and last[2] + b[2] <= WIDEST and across[b[1]:b[1] + b[3], b[0] - 1].all():
                    last[2] += b[2]
                elif not horizontal and last and last[0] == b[0] and last[2] == b[2] and last[1] + last[3] == b[1] \
                        and last[3] + b[3] <= WIDEST and down[b[1] - 1, b[0]:b[0] + b[2]].all():
                    last[3] += b[3]
                else:
                    out.append(list(b))
            return out

        for column, row, width, height in joined(joined(squares, True), False):
            number = len(rects)
            for r in range(row, row + height):
                for c in range(column, column + width):
                    owner[(r, c, cells[(r, c)])] = number
            block = z[row:row + height, column:column + width]
            rects.append([column, row, width, height, int(round(float(block.mean()) * 4)), kind, surface])
    return rects, owner, columns


def cuts(columns, parts, walls=None, kept=frozenset()):
    """Where a surface's rectangles must end: between two samples side by side where the surface goes on and one of
    its floors there is one way with another surface's floor in the other sample, through no other floor or by a way
    kept (bridges; the ground going on under a deck or a stair beside it). An opening must lie on the sides of both
    its rectangles: the game
    reads one on a tile on the neighbour's facing side too (Walk.lua), and a line pulled through it must keep to both.
    {(surface, side): {(row, column)}}: the side 1 cuts between a sample and the one east of it, the side 2 ones
    between a sample and the one south of it (rectangles, surfaces: parts)."""
    of = {(r, c, i): surface for surface, cells in enumerate(parts) for (r, c), i in cells.items()}
    found = {}
    for r in range(SIDE):
        for c in range(SIDE):
            here = columns[r][c]
            for side, dr, dc in SIDES[1:3]:
                nr, nc = r + dr, c + dc
                if nr >= SIDE or nc >= SIDE:
                    continue
                there = columns[nr][nc]
                if len(here) < 2 and len(there) < 2:
                    continue  # a surface goes on across only where one of the two holds another surface's floor too
                ours = {of.get((r, c, i)) for i in range(len(here))}
                theirs = {of.get((nr, nc, j)) for j in range(len(there))}
                heights, others = [f[0] for f in here], [f[0] for f in there]
                for i, a in enumerate(here):
                    for j, b in enumerate(there):
                        one, other = of.get((r, c, i)), of.get((nr, nc, j))
                        if (one == other or not near(a, b) or walled(walls, r, c, side, a[0], b[0])
                                or (through(heights, others, a[0], b[0]) and (r, c, i, nr, nc, j) not in kept)):
                            continue
                        for surface in {one} & theirs | {other} & ours:
                            found.setdefault((surface, side), set()).add((r, c))
    return found


def meets(a, b, side):
    """Whether rectangle b (on a's grid) lies right across a's side: its own facing side along a's."""
    if side == 0:
        return b[1] + b[3] == a[1]
    if side == 1:
        return b[0] == a[0] + a[2]
    if side == 2:
        return b[1] == a[1] + a[3]
    return b[0] + b[2] == a[0]


def openings(rects, owner, columns, borders, walls=None, kept=frozenset()):
    """The openings of every rectangle on its own tile: {rect: [[side, first, last, neighbour]]}: along each side,
    every floor beside it one way with its own (another surface's through no other floor, or by a way kept: bridges),
    of a rectangle that meets it there (cuts), so each opening is found from both; and what lies along the tile's
    edges for the next tiles: borders[side] = [(position, z, rect, walls)] per floor there, with the walls on the way
    to that side (rasterize)."""
    walls = walls or {}
    found = {number: [] for number in range(len(rects))}
    for number, (column, row, width, height, *_) in enumerate(rects):
        for side, dr, dc in SIDES:
            if side in (0, 2):
                cells = [(row if side == 0 else row + height - 1, c) for c in range(column, column + width)]
            else:
                cells = [(r, column + width - 1 if side == 1 else column) for r in range(row, row + height)]
            for r, c in cells:
                own = mine = None
                for i, floor in enumerate(columns[r][c]):
                    if owner.get((r, c, i)) == number:
                        own, mine = floor, i
                        break
                if own is None:
                    continue
                nr, nc = r + dr, c + dc
                position = c if side in (0, 2) else r
                if not (0 <= nr < SIDE and 0 <= nc < SIDE):
                    borders.setdefault(side, []).append((position, own[0], number, walls.get((r, c, side), ())))
                    continue
                for j, floor in enumerate(columns[nr][nc]):
                    other = owner.get((nr, nc, j))
                    if (other is not None and other != number and near(floor, own)
                            and not walled(walls, r, c, side, own[0], floor[0])
                            and meets(rects[number], rects[other], side)
                            and (rects[other][6] == rects[number][6] or (r, c, mine, nr, nc, j) in kept
                                 or not through([f[0] for f in columns[r][c]], [f[0] for f in columns[nr][nc]],
                                                own[0], floor[0]))):
                        found[number].append([side, position, position, other])
    return {number: merged(runs) for number, runs in found.items()}


def merged(runs):
    """A rectangle's openings, those on one side into one neighbour that meet or overlap joined into runs, in the
    order of their sides and first samples."""
    out = []
    for side, first, last, target in sorted(runs, key=lambda run: (run[0], run[3], run[1])):
        if out and out[-1][0] == side and out[-1][3] == target and first <= out[-1][2] + 1:
            out[-1][2] = max(out[-1][2], last)
        else:
            out.append([side, first, last, target])
    return sorted(out, key=lambda run: (run[0], run[1], run[3]))


def bridges(rects, owner, columns, links, walls=None):
    """The ways through another floor (through) a tile keeps: where floors one way with each other only so lie in
    parts no opening of the tile joins, the first such two floors (by row and column) between each two parts, each
    way round: {(row, column, index, row, column, index)}. A hall whose door is a wall in the walking data is reached
    under its floor, the inner ruins of Lordaeron from the ground under their courtyard."""
    parent = list(range(len(rects)))

    def find(node):
        while parent[node] != node:
            parent[node] = parent[parent[node]]
            node = parent[node]
        return node

    for number, runs in links.items():
        for *_, target in runs:
            parent[find(number)] = find(target)
    kept = set()
    for r in range(SIDE):
        for c in range(SIDE):
            here = columns[r][c]
            for side, dr, dc in SIDES[1:3]:
                nr, nc = r + dr, c + dc
                if nr >= SIDE or nc >= SIDE:
                    continue
                there = columns[nr][nc]
                if len(here) < 2 and len(there) < 2:
                    continue  # a way through another floor needs one in either sample
                heights, others = [f[0] for f in here], [f[0] for f in there]
                for i, a in enumerate(here):
                    for j, b in enumerate(there):
                        one, other = owner.get((r, c, i)), owner.get((nr, nc, j))
                        if (one is None or other is None or rects[one][6] == rects[other][6]
                                or find(one) == find(other) or not near(a, b)
                                or walled(walls, r, c, side, a[0], b[0]) or not through(heights, others, a[0], b[0])):
                            continue
                        kept |= {(r, c, i, nr, nc, j), (nr, nc, j, r, c, i)}
                        parent[find(one)] = find(other)
    return kept


def plan(alone, found, specks, built, walls=None):
    """A tile's rectangles, owner of every floor and floors (rectangles), its openings and borders (openings): with
    the ways through another floor its parts need (bridges), the rectangles cut again for them."""
    rects, owner, columns = rectangles(alone, found, specks, built, walls)
    borders = {}
    links = openings(rects, owner, columns, borders, walls)
    kept = bridges(rects, owner, columns, links, walls)
    if kept:
        rects, owner, columns = rectangles(alone, found, specks, built, walls, kept)
        borders = {}
        links = openings(rects, owner, columns, borders, walls, kept)
    return rects, owner, columns, links, borders


# -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  A tile
# -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
def sunken(triangles, height, hole):
    """Which triangles (in the tile's samples) lie wholly under the ground, where it has no hole: inside the earth, as
    a tree's roots or the foot of a rock."""
    rows = np.clip(triangles[..., 1].astype(np.int64), 0, SIDE - 1)
    columns = np.clip(triangles[..., 0].astype(np.int64), 0, SIDE - 1)
    return ((triangles[..., 2] < height[rows, columns] - JOIN) & ~hole[rows, columns]).all(axis=-1)


def tile(job):
    """Everything of one tile: (key, rects, openings, borders, open: the rectangles with the sky over some of their
    floors, buried: {rectangle: how many of its floors are buried}), or (key, None, ...) without ground."""
    key, folder, waters, liquids, near_places = job
    path = folder / "adt" / f"{key}.adt"
    if not path.exists():
        return key, None, None, None, None, None
    height, hole, water, closed, steep = ground(path, waters, liquids)
    row0, col0 = key % (GRID * GRID) // GRID, key % GRID
    north0, west0 = HALF - row0 * TILE, HALF - col0 * TILE
    spans, walls = {}, {}
    for where in near_places:
        if where["kind"] == DOODAD:
            corners = model(folder, where["file"])
        else:
            corners = building(folder, where["file"], where["sets"])
        if not len(corners):
            continue
        world = placed(where, corners)
        # In the tile's samples: column from the west edge, row from the north edge, height as it is.
        samples = np.stack([(west0 - world[..., 1]) / SAMPLE, (north0 - world[..., 0]) / SAMPLE, world[..., 2]], axis=-1)
        inside = ((samples[..., 0].max(axis=1) >= 0) & (samples[..., 0].min(axis=1) < SIDE)
                  & (samples[..., 1].max(axis=1) >= 0) & (samples[..., 1].min(axis=1) < SIDE))
        if where["kind"] == DOODAD:
            inside &= ~sunken(samples, height, hole)
        rasterize(samples[inside], spans, walls)
        # A building's water, magma and slime join the ground's (the higher where both are).
        for points, kind in pools(folder / "wmo", where["file"]) if where["kind"] == BUILDING else ():
            world = placed(where, points)
            column = np.floor((west0 - world[:, 1]) / SAMPLE).astype(np.int64)
            row = np.floor((north0 - world[:, 0]) / SAMPLE).astype(np.int64)
            inside = (row >= 0) & (row < SIDE) & (column >= 0) & (column < SIDE)
            np.maximum.at(water if kind in waters else closed, (row[inside], column[inside]),
                          world[inside, 2].astype(np.float32))
    alone, found, specks, built = floors(height, hole, water, closed, spans, walls, steep)
    rects, owner, columns, links, borders = plan(alone, found, specks, built, walls)
    if not rects:
        return key, None, None, None, None, None
    opened, buried = set(), {}
    for (r, c, i), number in owner.items():
        if columns[r][c][i][2] == SKY:
            opened.add(number)
        elif columns[r][c][i][2] == BURIED:
            buried[number] = buried.get(number, 0) + 1
    return key, rects, links, borders, opened, buried


# -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  Joining the tiles: openings across their edges, regions
# -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
NEXT = {0: (-1, 0, 2), 1: (0, 1, 3), 2: (1, 0, 0), 3: (0, -1, 1)}  # side: row step, column step, its opposite


def across(tiles):
    """The openings between tiles: for each edge, every two floors at the same place along it one way with each
    other, no wall on the way between them on either tile (each found from both, as on a tile: openings). Through
    another floor or not: on a tile's edge an opening lies on both its rectangles' sides however they lie."""
    for key, (rects, links, borders, *_) in tiles.items():
        continent, rest = divmod(key, GRID * GRID)
        row, column = divmod(rest, GRID)
        for side, (dr, dc, opposite) in NEXT.items():
            other = continent * GRID * GRID + (row + dr) * GRID + (column + dc)
            if other not in tiles or not (0 <= row + dr < GRID and 0 <= column + dc < GRID):
                continue
            theirs = {}
            for position, z, rect, walls in tiles[other][2].get(opposite, []):
                theirs.setdefault(position, []).append((z, rect, walls))
            runs = {}
            for position, z, rect, walls in borders.get(side, []):
                for their_z, their_rect, their_walls in theirs.get(position, []):
                    if abs(their_z - z) <= CLIMB and not stands(walls, z, their_z) and not stands(their_walls, z, their_z):
                        runs.setdefault(rect, []).append([side, position, position, their_rect + 65536 * (1 + side)])
            for rect, units in runs.items():
                links[rect] = merged(links[rect] + units)


def ends(path=TRAVEL):
    """Both ends of every lift and every portal in the master's set Travel: [(map, x, y, z)] in the world's yards (a
    lift's lower end first, a portal's trigger first)."""
    found = []
    if not path.exists():
        return found
    for text in path.read_text(encoding="utf-8").splitlines():
        line = json.loads(text) if text.strip() else {}
        way = line.get("path") or []
        if (line.get("kind") in ("lift", "portal") and len(way) >= 2 and "map" in way[0]
                and all("z" in point for point in way[:2])):
            found.extend((way[0]["map"], point["x"], point["y"], point["z"]) for point in way[:2])
    return found


def landings(tiles, ends):
    """The rectangles the lifts' and the portals' ends land on, as Navigation finds them (Walk.Landing): of those
    within LANDING yards whose height lies within RISE of the end, the nearest by the squares of both, the rise LEVEL
    times. {(key, number)}"""
    found = set()
    for world, x, y, z in ends:
        row, column = int((HALF - x) // TILE), int((HALF - y) // TILE)
        best = None
        for dr in (-1, 0, 1):
            for dc in (-1, 0, 1):
                key = world * GRID * GRID + (row + dr) * GRID + column + dc
                if key not in tiles or not (0 <= row + dr < GRID and 0 <= column + dc < GRID):
                    continue
                north0, west0 = HALF - (row + dr) * TILE, HALF - (column + dc) * TILE
                for number, (c, r, w, h, quarters, *_) in enumerate(tiles[key][0]):
                    north, south = north0 - r * SAMPLE, north0 - (r + h) * SAMPLE
                    west, east = west0 - c * SAMPLE, west0 - (c + w) * SAMPLE
                    gap = math.hypot(max(south - x, 0, x - north), max(east - y, 0, y - west))
                    rise = quarters / 4 - z
                    cost = gap * gap + (LEVEL * rise) ** 2
                    if gap <= LANDING and abs(rise) <= RISE and (best is None or cost < best[0]):
                        best = (cost, (key, number))
        if best:
            found.add(best[1])
    return found


def regions(tiles, keep=()):
    """A region number for every rectangle: one for all that can be walked to from each other; regions smaller
    than SMALLEST samples get none (nobody walks onto a roof or a ledge from the ground), nor those with the sky over
    none of their floors (a cellar the walking data closes: nobody gets in or out) or most of them buried (the ground
    under Stormwind's buildings, open only to the gaps between them), but those of the rectangles in keep (the lifts'
    and the portals' landings: Freewind Post's mesa, the Undercity, the Wizard's Sanctum's rooms). The numbers are
    given over the tiles of the run, in the keys' order: a run of a whole map gives the same numbers again, one of
    a few tiles (--only) numbers that cannot agree with the master's, so it writes no staging (main)."""
    parent = {}

    def find(node):
        while parent.get(node, node) != node:
            parent[node] = parent.get(parent[node], parent[node])
            node = parent[node]
        return node

    for key, (rects, links, *_) in tiles.items():
        continent, rest = divmod(key, GRID * GRID)
        row, column = divmod(rest, GRID)
        for number, list_ in links.items():
            for _, _, _, target in list_:
                where, index = divmod(target, 65536)
                other = key
                if where:
                    dr, dc, _ = NEXT[where - 1]
                    other = continent * GRID * GRID + (row + dr) * GRID + (column + dc)
                a, b = find((key, number)), find((other, index))
                if a != b:
                    parent[a] = b
    area, sky, deep = {}, set(), {}
    for key, (rects, _, _, *rest) in tiles.items():
        opened = rest[0] if rest else range(len(rects))  # made-up tiles: all open, none buried
        for number, rect in enumerate(rects):
            root = find((key, number))
            area[root] = area.get(root, 0) + rect[2] * rect[3]
        for number in opened:
            sky.add(find((key, number)))
        for number, count in (rest[1] if len(rest) > 1 else {}).items():
            root = find((key, number))
            deep[root] = deep.get(root, 0) + count
    landed = {find(node) for node in keep}
    numbers = {}
    found = {}
    for key in sorted(tiles):  # in the keys' order, not the order the tiles were read in
        rects = tiles[key][0]
        for number in range(len(rects)):
            root = find((key, number))
            if (area[root] < SMALLEST or root not in sky or 2 * deep.get(root, 0) > area[root]) and root not in landed:
                continue
            if root not in numbers:
                numbers[root] = len(numbers) + 1
            found[(key, number)] = numbers[root]
    return found


def kept(tiles, region_of):
    """Each tile's rectangles of the regions kept, numbered anew, their openings pointing at the new numbers."""
    renumber = {}
    for key, (rects, *_) in tiles.items():
        count = 0
        for number in range(len(rects)):
            if (key, number) in region_of:
                renumber[(key, number)] = count
                count += 1
    out = {}
    for key, (rects, links, *_) in tiles.items():
        continent, rest = divmod(key, GRID * GRID)
        row, column = divmod(rest, GRID)
        new_rects, new_links = [], {}
        for number, rect in enumerate(rects):
            if (key, number) not in region_of:
                continue
            index = len(new_rects)
            new_rects.append(rect + [region_of[(key, number)]])
            moved = []
            for side, first, last, target in links.get(number, []):
                where, other_index = divmod(target, 65536)
                other = key
                if where:
                    dr, dc, _ = NEXT[where - 1]
                    other = continent * GRID * GRID + (row + dr) * GRID + (column + dc)
                if (other, other_index) in renumber:
                    moved.append([side, first, last, where * 65536 + renumber[(other, other_index)]])
            new_links[index] = moved
        if new_rects:
            out[key] = (new_rects, new_links)
    return out


# -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  The block
# -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
def cbor(value):
    """A value in CBOR: whole numbers and arrays only."""
    if isinstance(value, int):
        major, number = (0, value) if value >= 0 else (1, -1 - value)
    else:
        major, number = 4, len(value)
    if number < 24:
        head = bytes([major << 5 | number])
    elif number < 256:
        head = bytes([major << 5 | 24, number])
    elif number < 65536:
        head = bytes([major << 5 | 25]) + struct.pack(">H", number)
    elif number < 2 ** 32:
        head = bytes([major << 5 | 26]) + struct.pack(">I", number)
    else:
        head = bytes([major << 5 | 27]) + struct.pack(">Q", number)
    if major == 4:
        return head + b"".join(cbor(item) for item in value)
    return head


def decode(data):
    """A value back from CBOR (cbor's whole numbers and arrays), as the game's DeserializeCBOR reads it: the one
    reader of the blocks, dev/tools/walk-gates' and map-layers' too (dev/tests/walk.py reads them on its own)."""
    at = 0

    def item():
        nonlocal at
        head = data[at]
        at += 1
        major, info = head >> 5, head & 31
        if info >= 24:
            size = {24: 1, 25: 2, 26: 4, 27: 8}[info]
            info, at = int.from_bytes(data[at:at + size], "big"), at + size
        if major == 0:
            return info
        if major == 1:
            return -1 - info
        return [item() for _ in range(info)]

    return item()


def shared(a, b, side, dr, dc):
    """The first and last sample of the edge two rectangles share on a side of a (b on the tile dr, dc tiles
    away), or None when they share none (b does not lie right across that side)."""
    ac, ar, aw, ah = a[0], a[1], a[2], a[3]
    bc, br, bw, bh = b[0] + dc * SIDE, b[1] + dr * SIDE, b[2], b[3]
    if not meets(a, (bc, br, bw, bh), side):
        return None
    if side in (0, 2):
        first, last = max(ac, bc), min(ac + aw, bc + bw) - 1
    else:
        first, last = max(ar, br), min(ar + ah, br + bh) - 1
    return (first, last) if first <= last else None


def block(rects, links, key, tiles):
    """A tile's block: [FORMAT, surfaces, rectangles, openings]. Its rectangles come surface by surface; a
    surface is written as how many rectangles it has, its kind and its region; a rectangle as its column, row,
    width, height and z. Two rectangles of one surface that share an edge are one way along all of it, as the
    game works out from the rectangles themselves; every other opening is written: along the whole edge two
    rectangles share as the neighbour alone, else as -1 - side, then the first and last sample, then the
    neighbour. The neighbour is its index times 5 plus where it lies (0 on the tile, else 1 + the next tile's
    direction). On its own tile an opening is written once, on the north or west side of the two (the game
    adds the other way when it unpacks the tile); into the next tile, from both."""
    flat_surfaces, flat_rects, flat_links = [], [], []
    for number, rect in enumerate(rects):
        column, row, width, height, z, kind, surface, region = rect
        if number == 0 or rects[number - 1][6] != surface:
            flat_surfaces.extend([0, kind, region])
        flat_surfaces[-3] += 1
        flat_rects.extend([column, row, width, height, z])
        out = []
        for side, first, last, target in links.get(number, []):
            where, index = divmod(target, 65536)
            if where == 0 and side in (1, 2):
                continue  # written from the other one
            dr, dc = (0, 0) if where == 0 else NEXT[where - 1][:2]
            other = tiles[key][0][index] if where == 0 else tiles[key + dr * GRID + dc][0][index]
            edge = shared(rect, other, side, dr, dc)
            whole = edge is not None and edge == (first, last)
            if whole and where == 0 and other[6] == surface:
                continue  # the game finds it from the rectangles
            if whole:
                out.append(index * 5 + where)
            else:
                out.extend([-1 - side, first, last, index * 5 + where])
        flat_links.append(len(out))
        flat_links.extend(out)
    packed = zlib.compressobj(9, zlib.DEFLATED, -15)
    data = packed.compress(cbor([FORMAT, flat_surfaces, flat_rects, flat_links])) + packed.flush()
    return base64.b64encode(data).decode("ascii")


